This is the first page of a typescript of Deligne's 'Courbes elliptiques : formulaire (d'après J. Tate)'. The typescript later appeared in LNM 476 (Modular Functions of One Variable IV, Antwerp), pp. 53–73, 1975, as the handwritten note on the page itself says. It sets up the relative framework.

A 'curve of genus 1' over a base scheme S is defined as follows:
- p : E → S is proper, flat and of finite presentation;
- it has a section e landing in the smooth locus;
- its geometric fibres are reduced, irreducible curves of arithmetic genus 1.

Since a fibre has a smooth rational point and arithmetic genus 1, it is either smooth of genus 1, or rational with a single singular point of δ-invariant 1. That singular point is unibranch (a cusp) or has two branches (an ordinary node). Hence the trichotomy:
- (a) elliptic: a smooth genus-1 curve;
- (b) multiplicative: P^1 with two distinct points glued, i.e. a nodal cubic, with smooth locus G_m;
- (c) additive: P^1 with two infinitely near points glued, i.e. a cuspidal cubic, with smooth locus G_a.

The Hodge bundle ω = e*Ω^1_{E/S} is introduced as an invertible sheaf on S. The page then begins noting that e is a relative effective Cartier divisor, which is used to build Weierstrass equations from O(ne).

The purpose is the formulary of Weierstrass coefficients a_i, b_i, c_4, c_6, Δ, j as sections of ω^{⊗k}, valid over any base (including characteristics 2 and 3). This is the setting of Deligne–Rapoport and Katz's p-adic modular forms. Grothendieck's archive copy (cote 93, with Gauss–Manin/Cartier material) carries his marginal 'G?' marks. One such mark, by 'arithmétique', seems to indicate he inserted the qualifier 'arithmetic', which is needed because fibres of type b) and c) have geometric genus 0.
